# When Does Prediction Pay Off? Replication package

Code, configuration and results for the paper "When Does Prediction Pay Off? A Sensitivity Analysis of Predictive versus Reactive Autoscaling under Controlled Burst Intensity and Provisioning Delay".

## Contents

```
src/sim.py              discrete time autoscaling simulator (policies, queue model, metrics)
src/workload.py         trace loading, calibration/test split, burst injection
src/run_experiments.py  full factorial sweep (7,600 runs) and forecaster calibration
src/analyse.py          medians, paired Wilcoxon tests, summary tables
src/figures.py          every figure in the paper
results/                raw per run results and summary tables (CSV, JSON)
figures/                figures as PDF and PNG
requirements.txt        Python dependencies
```

## Data

The workload is the Wikipedia per second request trace distributed with the RAS simulator by Quattrocchi et al. (IEEE TSC 2024, doi:10.1109/TSC.2024.3354062). It is **not redistributed here**. Download it with:

```
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/deib-polimi/RAS.git
cp RAS/generators/wiki.1190153705.csv data/wiki.csv
```

## Reproduce

```
pip install -r requirements.txt
mkdir -p results figures
python src/run_experiments.py data/wiki.csv results     # about 1 minute on 2 cores
python src/analyse.py results
python src/figures.py results figures data/wiki.csv
```

All runs are deterministic: seeds fix both the test segment and the burst positions.

## Key settings

Run length 3,600 s (first 300 s warm up), replica capacity 20 req/s, SLA 250 ms, client timeout 10 s, control period 15 s, metric window 30 s, target utilization 0.7, tolerance 0.1, scale down stabilization 300 s, scale up limit max(4, n). Factors: burst magnitude {1, 2, 4, 8}, duration {30, 120, 600} s, onset {10, 120} s, provisioning delay {0, 30, 60, 120, 300} s, 20 seeds. Forecaster parameters are tuned on the first 20 h of the trace only (see results/config_used.json).

